package Lessons

use Libraries.Web.Page.all
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.ComputerScienceStandards2026
use Libraries.Web.Page.Standards.QuorumStandards

class SceneComponents is QuorumPage
    // The site's sets of standards, built once for every page.
    QuorumStandards standards

    on create
        SetLocation("tutorials/Scenes/components.html")
    end

    action Setup
        // For search engines and link previews.
        SetDescription("Tour the parts of Quorum Studio's scene editor: the palette, the editor and its grids, the Properties panel and the terrain editor.")

        Title("What does a Scene have in it?")

        Overview()
        WarmUp()
        Explore()
        Engage()
        WrapUp()
    end

    action Overview
        Section("Overview")
        Paragraph("Scenes have palettes and editors. You use them to add, delete, and change items in
your scene. This tutorial tours the parts of Quorum Studio's scene editor: the Scene and Palette tabs, the
editor with its grid, and the Properties panel. It walks through what the palette offers, from simple shapes
to whole kits of 3D models and terrain. It also gives a first look at the terrain editor, and explains how
positions work on the 2D and 3D grids, since every item you place has one.")

        Subsection("Goals")
        Goals()
        ListItem("Name the parts of the scene editor and say what each is for.")
        ListItem("Describe what is in the palette, for 2D and 3D scenes.")
        ListItem("Get around the scene editor, and the terrain editor, with the keyboard or the mouse.")
        ListItem("Work out an item's position from its grid square, in 2D and in 3D.")
    end

    action WarmUp
        Section("Warm up")
        Paragraph("Stand up and point straight ahead, then straight up, then to your right. Those are
three directions. If you had to tell someone exactly where a cup is in your classroom, using only three
numbers, where would you start counting from, and what unit would you use?")

        Subsection("Vocabulary")
        Paragraph("You will be learning about the following vocabulary words:")
        Table("Vocabulary", "media/scenes/Components/Vocabulary.csv")

        Subsection("CSTA Standards")
        ComputerScienceStandards2026 computerScience = standards:GetComputerScience2026()
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_SOFTWARE_DEVELOPMENT_DESIGN_AND_DEVELOPMENT_3)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:HIGH_SCHOOL_PROGRAM_DEVELOPMENT_14)
        CoversStandard(computerScience, computerScience:SPECIALTY_1_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_X_PLUS_COMPUTER_SCIENCE_4)
        StandardsList(computerScience)
    end

    action Explore
        Section("Explore")
        PalettesAndEditors()
        GettingAround()
        KeyboardBasics()
        PaletteContents()
        Grid2D()
        Grid3D()
        TerrainEditor()
    end

    private action PalettesAndEditors
        Subsection("Palettes and editors")
        Paragraph("Scenes have several parts. First, they have a palette. The palette is where you
find built-in art assets to use for a game. You can get your own, but this gives you a place to start. Second,
they have an editor. The editor lets you add, delete, and change things for your game. Third, beside the
editor, there is a Properties panel that shows the details of whatever is selected.")
    end

    private action GettingAround
        Subsection("Getting around")
        Paragraph("When a scene is open, three tabs sit together on the left of Quorum Studio: "):Strong("Projects"):Text(", "):Strong("Scene"):Text(" and "):Strong("Palette"):Text(". The editor fills the middle, and the Properties panel is on its right.")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("The Scene tab"):Text(" is a tree of everything in the scene. Each layer, such as Default Layer3D, has a
branch. Under it, "):Strong("Cameras"):Text(" holds the "):Strong("Editor Camera"):Text(", and "):Strong("Items"):Text(" holds the items in that layer. In it, we can choose a layer, find items, select them
or delete them.")
        ListItem():Strong("The Palette tab"):Text(" is a tree of the items you can place into a 2D or 3D scene. Choose an item and
press Enter to put a copy of it at the editor's cursor, or drag it into the scene with the mouse. "):Link("Adding and Removing Items", "/tutorials/Scenes/items.html"):Text(" explains this in detail.")
        ListItem():Strong("The Properties panel"):Text(" shows the selected item's properties, such as its name, position, scale, rotation and
color. With nothing selected, it says "):DoubleQuote():Output("Nothing selected"):DoubleQuote():Text(". You can drag the bar between the
editor and the panel to make the panel wider or narrower.")

        Table("Keys for the tabs and the Properties panel", "media/scenes/Components/Keys.csv")
        Paragraph("You can also click the tabs and the panel with the mouse.")
    end

    private action KeyboardBasics
        Subsection("Getting around the editor with the keyboard")
        Paragraph("Everything in the scene editor can be done from the keyboard, and a screen reader hears
what happens. Here is the general pattern; the "):Link("Navigation and Keys", "/tutorials/Scenes/navigate.html"):Text(" page lists every key.")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("The cursor."):Text(" When nothing is selected, the arrow keys move a cursor around the
grid. In 3D, Up and Down move it forward and back, and Ctrl+Up and Ctrl+Down (Cmd on a Mac) move it up and
down. When you let go of an arrow key, a screen reader says what is at the cursor and where, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Cube, 3 1 0"):DoubleQuote():Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("Placing."):Text(" In the Palette tab, choose an item and press Enter. A preview of it
appears at the cursor. Move it with the arrow keys, then press Enter to place it, or Escape to leave without
placing it. Quorum Studio says "):DoubleQuote():Output("Placed"):DoubleQuote():Text(", followed by the item's name.")
        ListItem():Strong("Selecting."):Text(" Press Enter on the cursor to select what is there. If more than
one item shares the spot, Tab and Shift+Tab move between them. Escape leaves the selection.")
        ListItem():Strong("Changing."):Text(" With an item selected, the arrow keys move it. Alt with =, - and
letter keys change its size, and Alt with the arrows turns it (Option on a Mac). For exact values, press
Ctrl+P (Cmd+P) to go to the Properties panel and type a number in its fields.")

        Paragraph("With the mouse, you can click an item to select it, drag items from the palette
into the scene, and move the 3D camera: hold the right button and drag to look around, hold the middle button
and drag to orbit, hold Shift and the middle button to pan, and use the scroll wheel to zoom.")
    end

    private action PaletteContents
        Subsection("What is in the palette")
        Paragraph("The palette is a tree of groups. Which groups you see depends on the kind of scene.")

        Paragraph("For "):Strong("3D"):Text(" scenes:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Basic 3D"):Text(", the building blocks:")
        NestedBulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Cube"):Text(", "):Strong("Sphere"):Text(" and "):Strong("Cylinder"):Text(", each 1 meter wide, tall and deep, which you can stretch into walls, floors, posts and more;")
        ListItem():Strong("Point Light"):Text(", a light at one point that shines in every direction;")
        ListItem():Strong("Audio3D"):Text(", a sound that plays from a spot in the scene, so it sounds louder
as the camera gets closer;")
        ListItem():Strong("Terrain"):Text(", ground for the scene. It starts flat, 128 by 128 meters, and
you shape it with the terrain editor (below).")
        EndNestedList()
        ListItem():Strong("Custom 3D"):Text(", for your own art. "):Strong("Custom Model"):Text(" loads a 3D model from an OBJ file in your project. "):Strong("Custom Cube"):Text(", "):Strong("Custom Sphere"):Text(" and "):Strong("Custom Cylinder"):Text(" are shapes you can cover with your own picture.")
        ListItem():Strong("One group for each kit"):Text(" in your project's "):Code("Resources/Tilesets3D"):Text(" folder. A new 3D project has eight kits, with hundreds of models between them:")
        NestedBulletList()
        ListItem("city roads, with bridges, lights and construction pieces;")
        ListItem("a graveyard, with stone walls, urns, skeletons, zombies and vampires;")
        ListItem("hexagon tiles for board-style maps, such as grass, water, sand, rivers, paths and buildings;")
        ListItem("holiday scenery, such as snowy pine trees, wreaths and a toy train;")
        ListItem("nature, such as trees, cliffs, rocks, flowers, crops, fences and bridges;")
        ListItem("medieval castles, with walls, floors, columns and battlements;")
        ListItem("space, such as rockets, hangars, corridors, platforms and a monorail;")
        ListItem("tower defense, with towers, weapons, enemies and ground tiles.")

        Paragraph("For "):Strong("2D"):Text(" scenes:")

        BulletList()
        ListItem():Strong("Primitives 2D"):Text(": "):Strong("Rectangle"):Text(" and "):Strong("Circle"):Text(".")
        ListItem():Strong("One group for each tileset"):Text(" in "):Code("Resources/Tilesets"):Text(". A new 2D project has three: "):Strong("Characters 2D"):Text(", a boy and a girl in walking and running poses; "):Strong("Dungeon 2D"):Text(", with walls, potions, weapons, treasure chests and monsters; and "):Strong("Nature Summer 2D"):Text(", with grass, water, dirt, stone, trees, rocks and the edge tiles that join them.")

        Paragraph("The default art is open licensed and can be used freely in any project as a starting
point. The 3D kits come with a "):Code("License.txt"):Text(" file that says so. Even when art is free to use, it is good practice to say where it
came from in your game's credits. All default art is child-appropriate and should be usable in or out of a
classroom.")

        Paragraph("With a screen reader, the palette is a tree: you hear each group's name, such as
Basic 3D, and then each item's name, such as Cube or Terrain, with its description.")
    end

    private action Grid2D
        Subsection("The 2D grid editor")
        Paragraph("Modern computer gaming uses both 2D and 3D graphics. To say the least, 2D is
simpler for many reasons, especially the math behind the scenes. The metaphor used in many scene editors is
one of a grid. In 2D, we have x and y coordinates, and in 3D we add z. This means that for 2D, we can add items
at any horizontal and vertical value. Positions don't have to be whole numbers: 1.5 works as well as 1.")
        Paragraph("Consider an example. In the image below, there are horizontal and vertical lines.
They are drawn on the screen for the editor, but do not show up in your game. By default, these lines are
spaced 32 units apart, which for the 2D editor is pixels. This default is used because many open-source art
tilesets use a fixed size, often 32 or 16 pixel squares, for map layout.")
        Paragraph("The image also has a green section, which is a layout a person added for their
game. In this case, the tiles are just images in a row, but this shows why scene editors help. Writing down
each coordinate of each item in code takes a long time. Having a tool that lets you place them is easier. In
the editor, each tile can then be adjusted in many ways.")
        Image("media/scenes/CreateScene/Palette2D.png", "The 2D grid in the scene editor, with a row of green grass tiles and the Properties panel on the right.")
        Paragraph("The math to find the position of each square on the grid can be a little tricky.
Suppose the grid unit is P, where P is 32 pixels. To find a square's position, we multiply its grid column and
row by P. For example, the square at grid position (10, 10) is at (P × 10, P × 10), or (320, 320). This is
important when you set an item's position in the Properties panel.")
        Paragraph("You can turn grid snapping on and off, and make its squares larger or smaller:")
        Table("Keys for the grid", "media/scenes/Components/GridKeys.csv")
    end

    private action Grid3D
        Subsection("The 3D grid editor")
        Paragraph("Placing items in 3D is similar to 2D. Just like in 2D, you can choose items and put
them in the scene at an x, y, and now z position. Just like before, x means left and right and y means up and
down. z means forward (positive) or backward (negative). In a new scene, the game's camera starts a little
above and behind the center of the scene, at x 0, y 3 and z -5, looking forward, toward positive z, and
slightly down.")
        Paragraph("The axes stay where they are when the camera moves. If you turn the camera around,
positive z is still the same direction in the world, even though it now points toward you on screen. That the
camera can be pointed in any direction is confusing, but important. In computer science, the rule Quorum uses
for z is called a left-handed coordinate system. Different areas of science use different rules for z; for
example, physics usually uses a right-handed system, and pilots often count z going down.")
        Paragraph("One common sense way to think about the z coordinate in 3D is to put your arm in
front of you. That is a positive z, if you are the camera looking out. Putting your arm behind you is a
negative z. In graphics, if an item is in front of the camera, moving it farther away makes it look smaller,
and moving it closer makes it look larger. Too far away, or behind the camera, and the item disappears. A
camera has a position, and it also has a direction it looks in. For example, it might be at one x, y, z
position and looking at a different one.")
        Paragraph("Consider the image below. In it, there are walls and a spaceship. The editor camera
is pointed at the spaceship from above, at an angle. The grid lines run along the x and z axes, forming a flat
plane, like a floor. The up and down axis, y, has no grid lines, but it uses the same units. By default, the
lines are 1 unit apart. In 2D, pixels have no physical meaning, but in 3D, one unit is a meter. Because of
this, the properties you set on items, such as mass, and the real-time physics in your game mean the same
thing they would in the physical world.")
        Image("media/scenes/CreateScene/Palette3D.png", "The 3D grid in the scene editor, with walls and a spaceship seen from above at an angle.")
        Paragraph("The camera in the editor is the editor camera. Your game can use any camera it
wants, so if you want your game to start with the same view as the editor, you need to set the game's camera
that way.")
        Paragraph("The math for 3D is the same idea as 2D, with one more number. With the default grid
unit of 1 meter, the square at grid position (10, 10, 10) is at (10, 10, 10). If you make the grid larger so
that each square is P = 2 meters, the same square is at (P × 10, P × 10, P × 10), or (20, 20, 20).")
    end

    private action TerrainEditor
        Subsection("The terrain editor")
        Paragraph("Most outdoor 3D scenes need ground, and flat ground is just no fun. The "):Term("terrain editor"):Text(" is the part of the scene editor that shapes a terrain: raising hills, digging valleys,
painting grass or rock, and making the world bigger. It works like a sculpting tool. You choose a "):Strong("tool"):Text(", and a circle called the "):Term("brush"):Text(" appears on the ground. Wherever you use the brush, the tool changes the ground under it.")

        Paragraph("To start, add a Terrain from Basic 3D and select it. The Properties panel then shows the
terrain's sections: "):Strong("Terrain"):Text(" (its size and name), "):Strong("Materials"):Text(" (what the ground looks like), "):Strong("Brush"):Text(" (the tool and its size and strength), "):Strong("Generate"):Text(" (making ground from rules, such as rolling hills or mountains) and "):Strong("Physics"):Text(" (how things in a game stand on it).")

        Paragraph():Strong("With the mouse,"):Text(" the brush follows the pointer over the ground. Press the left button and drag to use the
tool, the way you'd drag a paintbrush.")

        Paragraph():Strong("With the keyboard,"):Text(" choose a tool with Alt and a number (Option on a Mac). The arrow keys then move the
brush instead of the terrain, relative to the way the camera faces. Hold Space to use the tool. Each time you
let go of an arrow key, Quorum Studio says the height of the ground under the brush, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Height 2.4"):DoubleQuote():Text(". After each use, it says what changed, such as "):DoubleQuote():Output("Raised by up to 2.4"):DoubleQuote():Text(".")

        Table("Keys for the terrain editor", "media/scenes/Components/TerrainKeys.csv")

        Paragraph("Every use of a tool can be undone and redone like any other change, and saving the scene
saves the terrain. The terrain pages later in this series, starting with "):Link("Terrain in a Scene", "/tutorials/Scenes/terrain.html"):Text(", cover each tool in detail.")
    end

    action Engage
        Section("Engage")
        Paragraph("In this activity, you and a partner build a small piece of terrain out of real
objects, place things on it, and record where they are, just as the terrain editor and the scene file do.
Gather a few sturdy objects from around the room to make the shape of the land, such as books, boxes, a
backpack or a rolled-up sweater. Arrange them on a table or the floor to make hills, a valley and some flat
ground. If you can, drape a large sheet of paper, a tablecloth or a sheet over them, so the ground is one
smooth surface, the way a real terrain is. Then mark one corner of the area as the origin, and lay a ruler or
measuring tape along two edges: the edge running to your right is the x-axis, the edge running away from you
is the z-axis, and straight up is the y-axis. Agree on a unit, such as centimeters.")
        Paragraph("Now place at least three small items on your terrain, such as a coin, an eraser and
a game piece. Put at least one on a hilltop and one in a low spot. For each item, write down its name and its
x, y and z coordinates: measure x and z along the edges from the origin, and measure y as the height from the
table or floor up to the bottom of the item. Notice that, on terrain, an item's y depends on where it sits: the
same coin has a different y on a hill than in a valley. When you're done, trade lists with another pair and
check one of their items by measuring it yourself. Then discuss: did you measure to the nearest centimeter, or
the nearest half centimeter? How would your list change if you only measured to the nearest 10 centimeters, and
what would you lose? A terrain in Quorum stores its heights at points 1 meter apart, so it makes the same kind
of choice.")
    end

    action WrapUp
        Section("Wrap up")
        Paragraph("Every item in a scene has a position, and the grid is a tool for choosing it. On
flat ground, an item's y is the same everywhere, but on terrain it depends on the shape of the land, which is
why the terrain editor reports the height under the brush. Why do you think the 3D editor uses meters instead
of pixels, and what would go wrong in a physics game if one unit had no real-world size?")
    end
end
